//@version=6
// Nova Statistical Filtering Oscillator - A statistical price oscillator that identifies overbought/oversold conditions
// using Z-Score normalization and dynamic volatility bands. The indicator smooths the Z-Score with TEMA
// to reduce noise and provides reversal signals when momentum returns from extreme levels.
indicator("Nova Statistical Filtering Oscillator [Pineify]", "Pineify - NSFO", overlay = false)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// INPUTS - User-configurable parameters for the indicator
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Statistical Window: Number of bars used to calculate the mean and standard deviation for Z-Score computation
// A longer period provides more stable readings but responds slower to price changes
lengthInput = input.int(20, "Statistical Window", minval = 2, tooltip = "Lookback period for the statistical evaluation.")
// Smoothing Length: TEMA period to reduce noise in the Z-Score, higher values = smoother but more lag
smoothInput = input.int(10, "Smoothing Length", minval = 1, tooltip = "Reduces noise using a zero-lag TEMA.")
// Bands Multiplier: Controls the width of the dynamic bands (in standard deviations of the smoothed oscillator)
// Higher values create wider bands, reducing false signals but missing some opportunities
multInput = input.float(2.0, "Bands Multiplier", minval = 0.5, step = 0.5, tooltip = "Multiplier for the dynamic overbought/oversold bands.")

// Color inputs for bullish (oversold to normal) and bearish (overbought to normal) states
bullColor = input.color(#089981, "Bullish Color", inline = "colors")  // Green - indicates upward momentum
bearColor = input.color(#F23645, "Bearish Color", inline = "colors")  // Red - indicates downward momentum

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FUNCTIONS - Custom functions used throughout the indicator
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Triple Exponential Moving Average (TEMA): An advanced smoothing technique that combines three EMAs
// to achieve smoother results with less lag compared to a simple EMA or SMA.
// Formula: TEMA = 3*EMA1 - 3*EMA2 + EMA3, where EMA1, EMA2, EMA3 are successive EMAs
// Parameters:
//   - src: The source data series (typically price or Z-Score)
//   - len: The lookback period for EMA calculation
tema(float src, int len) =>
    ema1 = ta.ema(src, len)
    ema2 = ta.ema(ema1, len)
    ema3 = ta.ema(ema2, len)
    3 * (ema1 - ema2) + ema3

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CALCULATIONS - Core statistical processing pipeline
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Z-Score Calculation - Statistical normalization of current price
// Z-Score measures how many standard deviations the current price is from the average (mean)
// Formula: Z = (Current Price - Mean) / Standard Deviation
// Interpretation:
//   - Z > 0: Price is above average (potentially overbought)
//   - Z < 0: Price is below average (potentially oversold)
//   - Z = ±2 or more: Statistically extreme (rare, ~5% of the time)
// Calculate the Simple Moving Average (mean) of closing prices over the lookback period
meanPrice = ta.sma(close, lengthInput)
// Calculate the standard deviation (volatility) of closing prices over the lookback period
stdevPrice = ta.stdev(close, lengthInput)
// Z-Score: Normalized price position. When stdev is zero (unusual market), default to 0 to avoid division errors
zScore = stdevPrice == 0 ? 0 : (close - meanPrice) / stdevPrice

// STEP 2: Smoothing the Z-Score - Apply TEMA to reduce market noise and choppy signals
// The raw Z-Score can be volatile; TEMA provides smoother transitions while maintaining responsiveness
smoothedStat = tema(zScore, smoothInput)

// STEP 3: Dynamic Bands - Create adaptive overbought/oversold thresholds
// These bands are based on the volatility of the smoothed oscillator itself, not the raw price
// This self-referential approach adapts to changing market conditions automatically
// Calculate the standard deviation of the smoothed oscillator to measure its typical range
bandStdev = ta.stdev(smoothedStat, lengthInput)
// Upper band: Positive threshold for overbought condition (upper extreme)
upperBand = bandStdev * multInput
// Lower band: Negative threshold for oversold condition (lower extreme)
lowerBand = -bandStdev * multInput

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VISUALS & PLOTS - Rendering the indicator on the chart
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gradient Colors: Create a dynamic color that transitions from bearish to bullish
// based on the oscillator's position relative to the dynamic bands
// When near lower band = more bearish color, when near upper band = more bullish color
oscColor = color.from_gradient(smoothedStat, lowerBand, upperBand, bearColor, bullColor)

// Plotting Dynamic Extreme Bands - Visual reference for overbought/oversold levels
// The upper band represents the overbought threshold (price extended too far upward)
// The lower band represents the oversold threshold (price extended too far downward)
// Both bands use semi-transparent colors for subtle visual reference
plot(upperBand, "Upper Band", color = color.new(bearColor, 50), style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1)
plot(lowerBand, "Lower Band", color = color.new(bullColor, 50), style = plot.style_line, linewidth = 1)
// Baseline at zero represents the statistical mean (average price)
baseline = plot(0, "Baseline", color = color.new(color.gray, 50))

// Plotting the main oscillator line and histogram representation
// Main oscillator: The smoothed Z-Score with gradient coloring
oscPlot = plot(smoothedStat, "Nova Oscillator", color = oscColor, linewidth = 2, style = plot.style_line)
// Histogram: Semi-transparent version of the oscillator for enhanced visibility
plot(smoothedStat, "Histogram", color = color.new(oscColor, 70), style = plot.style_histogram)

// Advanced Gradient Fill: Fills the area between oscillator and baseline
// Creates a visual distinction between bullish (above zero) and bearish (below zero) zones
// Uses varying transparency levels to create depth effect
fill(oscPlot, baseline, math.max(smoothedStat, 0), math.min(smoothedStat, 0), smoothedStat > 0 ? color.new(bullColor, 50) : color.new(bearColor, 100), smoothedStat > 0 ? color.new(bullColor, 100) : color.new(bearColor, 50))

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TRADING SIGNALS - Generate entry signals based on statistical reversal patterns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reversal Signal Logic: When the oscillator reaches an extreme level (band) and then
// crosses back toward the center, it suggests momentum is reversing from that extreme.
// This is based on the mean reversion principle - prices tend to return to their average.

// Bull Signal (Buy): Triggered when oscillator crosses ABOVE the lower band
// Indicates oversold condition is reversing - bullish momentum returning from statistical extreme
bullSignal = ta.crossover(smoothedStat, lowerBand)

// Bear Signal (Sell): Triggered when oscillator crosses BELOW the upper band
// Indicates overbought condition is reversing - bearish momentum returning from statistical extreme
bearSignal = ta.crossunder(smoothedStat, upperBand)

// Plotting the signals as circular markers on the chart at the signal location
// Buy signal: Green circle at the oscillator value (or na if no signal)
plotshape(bullSignal ? smoothedStat : na, "Buy Signal", shape.circle, location.absolute, bullColor, size = size.tiny)
// Sell signal: Red circle at the oscillator value (or na if no signal)
plotshape(bearSignal ? smoothedStat : na, "Sell Signal", shape.circle, location.absolute, bearColor, size = size.tiny)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ALERTS - Enable automated notifications when signals occur
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// These alert conditions can be configured in TradingView to send notifications
// when the indicator detects a statistical reversal opportunity
alertcondition(bullSignal, "Buy Signal", "Bullish momentum is returning from an oversold statistical extreme.")
alertcondition(bearSignal, "Sell Signal", "Bearish momentum is returning from an overbought statistical extreme.")
